WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:07.249
It's easy to look around these days and think
to yourself, "Humanity is screwed".

00:00:07.249 --> 00:00:11.960
The earth is getting hotter, the planet’s
drowning in plastic, and we’re on the brink

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.170
of a global energy crisis.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:18.800
The human population is growing faster than
we can feed and accommodate it, and we’re

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:25.180
entering an era of pandemic viruses, the most
dangerous of which seems to be stupidity.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:31.050
Things look bleak, but before you start washing
your Prozac down with hemlock, there is hope

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.630
on the horizon.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:38.640
Because a growing body of research is revealing
that a weird, ancient and freely occurring

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:45.040
life form could help us solve many of the
problems we face as a species today.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.100
And that life form is fungus.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:53.520
Now, when you hear the word 'fungus' you probably
think of mouldy bread or that stuff growing

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.500
on your big toe you're too afraid to see the
doctor about.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.719
But, thankfully, fungi are more diverse than
that.

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:08.950
Depending on who you speak to, there are between
2.2 and 5 million different species of fungi

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.840
in the world today, and together they form
an entirely separate kingdom, making them

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.380
neither plant nor animal nor bacteria.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:23.630
And though most of us tend to think of plants
and fungi as broadly similar, weirdly enough

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:30.050
fungi are actually more closely related to
us humans than they are to plants.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.860
That really does help explain some people,
doesn't it?

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:40.090
Fungi also include microorganisms like yeasts
and moulds, which are pretty much everywhere.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:44.820
They exist in the ocean, on land and in every
natural environment.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:48.860
They're on your coffee mug, they're on the
International Space Station.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:52.680
They're all over you and inside you right
now.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.540
You have my permission to freak out a little
bit.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:00.260
The fungal element most people are familiar
with is the mushroom, which is essentially

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.090
the fruit of a fungus.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:08.860
We’ve identified about 20 000 species of
fungi that produce mushrooms and, as we shall

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:14.200
see later, these vary from the deadly to the
delicious to the magical.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.210
But the mushroom is only the tip of the fungal
iceberg.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:24.470
Below lies a mysterious and often unseen web
of fibrous threads that weave over, in and

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.540
through organic matter.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.790
This is mycelium, the vegetative part of fungus.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:37.740
Mycelia help serve fungi's number one role
in the natural ecosystem: eating all the dead

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:38.740
stuff.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:43.290
Fungi can't photosynthesise like plants do
because they don't contain chlorophyll.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:49.290
So, like humans, they need to get their nutrients
from other organisms.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:57.170
They do this by decomposing organic compounds:
animals, wood, plants, basically anything

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:58.600
made from carbon.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:04.990
But, unlike humans and other beasts who eat
their food and then digest it, the mycelium

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:12.000
likes to do things the other way around, secreting
enzymes into or onto whatever it’s having

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:19.360
for dinner , to break it down into smaller
biological units before absorbing the nutrients.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:25.480
As if decomposing the world's organic material
wasn't enough of a responsibility, mycelium

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.840
plays another extraordinary role in natural
systems.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.120
Spreading throughout the soil, mycelia connect
with each other and the roots of plants and

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.200
trees to form complex communication and distribution
networks.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.900
When a fungus colonises the roots of a plant,
it sets up a win-win relationship known as

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:52.650
a "mycorrhiza" By connecting with plant root
systems, fungi receive carbohydrates, while

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:58.530
in return the plants receive extra water and
nutrients like phosphorus and nitrogen via

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.770
the fungi's mycelium.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:07.230
About 90% of land plants take advantage of
this set up, which also helps plants build

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:11.870
up their immune systems, making them stronger
and encouraging growth.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:16.410
When mycelium first connects to a plant's
root system, it triggers a defensive chemical

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.410
response in the plant.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:25.070
This process, called priming, makes plants'
immunity reactions faster and more efficient.

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.690
But the mycelia impact goes far beyond single
plants.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:36.050
We now know mycelium networks join up, connecting
plants with each other in a subterranean information

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:37.560
superhighway.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.520
Trees use the mycelium network to transfer
carbon, nitrogen and phosphorus between them,

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.880
helping maintain nutrient balance across many
different plants.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:52.940
It appears older trees even help younger ones
to grow - a study of seedlings growing in

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:58.650
the shade where they are more likely to be
short of nutrients revealed they received

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:02.090
carbon from donor trees around them via mycelium.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:09.090
And though these discoveries are still pretty
new, it seems plants can even use the mycelium

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.660
net to communicate.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:16.210
Studies have shown that when a single plant
is infected with a harmful fungus or attacked

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:22.880
by pests, it sends out a warning via the mycelium
network, with plants connected to that network

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.880
showing a more potent immune resistance.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:31.490
Plants that aren’t connected never get the
message and show weaker immune responses.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:37.670
By studying these stange and complex interactions,
scientists believe we may be able to dramatically

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:44.040
improve farming techniques that have traditionally
destroyed these precious mycelium networks,

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.650
which - along with the rise in cultivation
of edible mushrooms in third world countries,

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:52.080
could help in the fight against world hunger.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.360
But it's not all holding hands and “love
thy neighbour”.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:02.990
Some plants turn the mycelium internet into
the dark web by using it for nefarious purposes.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:09.570
Some orchids, for example, have little or
no ability to photosynthesise, so they hack

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:15.080
into surrounding plants via mycelium to steal
the carbon they need.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.450
Other plants are even more mercenary.

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:22.540
When competing for resources like water and
light, they use mycelium to release chemicals

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:27.510
into the soil that harm rivals or deter them
from growing.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:32.699
This process, called "allelopathy", is known
to happen via tree leaves and roots, but the

00:06:32.699 --> 00:06:36.320
discovery that fungi are helping to amplify
the effect is new.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:42.090
As a reminder of why they weren't invited
to the cool parties at college, many biologists

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:50.240
have started calling this dense communication
network the "wood wide web", and some estimate

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:57.240
there to be three hundred miles of mycelium
under every footstep you take through the

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:58.290
forest.

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:04.740
Nicknamed the humongous fungus, the largest
living organism on the planet is proof of

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:05.740
this.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:13.440
Growing in ancient woodland in Oregon is a
single giant fungus estimated to be 2,400

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:14.440
years old.

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:20.570
The mycelium of this armillaria ostoyae, popularly
known as the honey mushroom, covers an area

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:26.740
as big as 1,600 football fields and is more
than 5 kilometres across.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:31.900
It extends an average of one metre into the
earth, but above ground the only evidence

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:38.110
of its existence are clumps of honey mushrooms
that sporadically bloom after it’s rained.

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:43.290
For human beings, mycelium has other uses
which may be critical to getting out of the

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:45.389
mess we've created for ourselves.

00:07:45.389 --> 00:07:50.550
The fantastic qualities of different fungi,
especially their ability to break down carbon-based

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:57.000
substances, promise potential miracle solutions
to many of the environmental problems we’re

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.000
facing.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:03.070
And it's impossible to talk about these possibilities
without mentioning Paul Stamets.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:09.960
Paul is a mushroom hunter, mushroom cultivator,
and mycologist - someone who loves mushrooms

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:12.310
so much they study them as a job.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:17.290
He even wears a hat made of mushrooms, and
that should tell you all you need to know.

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:23.949
Paul and his team have grown rare strains
of fungi which have seemingly mystical medicinal

00:08:23.949 --> 00:08:29.290
properties while also discovering new potential
for well-known mushrooms.

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:33.019
Oyster mushrooms, for example, could help
clean up oil spills.

00:08:33.019 --> 00:08:38.390
Stamets has developed a strain that is saltwater
resistant and eats hydrocarbons, the organic

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:41.860
compounds in petroleum and natural gas.

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:47.899
One trial showed an oyster mushroom strain
could reduce diesel contaminants in soil from

00:08:47.899 --> 00:08:53.730
10,000 parts per million to just 200 parts
per million in about four months.

00:08:53.730 --> 00:09:00.540
The mycelium of the whimsically-named stump
fairy helmet mushroom can break down PCBs,

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:05.699
cancer-causing chemicals once used in the
manufacture of various electronic equipment.

00:09:05.699 --> 00:09:10.959
In fact, fungi could be the solution to one
of the biggest environmental contaminants

00:09:10.959 --> 00:09:13.439
we have: plastic.

00:09:13.439 --> 00:09:20.259
There are estimated to be more than 150 million
tonnes of plastic in our oceans, and by 2050

00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:22.350
there might be more plastic than fish.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:27.379
The problem with plastic is it takes forever
to break down and usually creates toxic pollution

00:09:27.379 --> 00:09:28.779
in the process.

00:09:28.779 --> 00:09:34.670
But in the last few years, scientists around
the world have discovered more than 50 types

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:37.639
of fungus that can eat plastic.

00:09:37.639 --> 00:09:45.629
The fungus pestalotiopsis microspora is capable
of surviving entirely on polyurethane - the

00:09:45.629 --> 00:09:51.129
main ingredient in plastic - and can even
break plastics down into new, safe fungal

00:09:51.129 --> 00:09:52.129
tissues.

00:09:52.129 --> 00:09:56.449
Mycelium isn’t just getting rid of the plastic
we’ve already produced, either - it’s

00:09:56.449 --> 00:10:01.649
also being used to create alternatives to
plastic packaging, which can take thousands

00:10:01.649 --> 00:10:03.889
of years to biodegrade.

00:10:03.889 --> 00:10:09.159
Beyond packaging, mycelium is a surprisingly
versatile and durable material in general

00:10:09.159 --> 00:10:15.069
- the mouldy spores that grow on agricultural
waste, or on old cardboard, are being converted

00:10:15.069 --> 00:10:20.079
into faux leather, textiles, furniture, and
even coffins.

00:10:20.079 --> 00:10:26.139
Mycelium composites can also be made strong
enough for use as a building material in the

00:10:26.139 --> 00:10:31.279
form of bricks, meaning there are implications
for the construction industry too.

00:10:31.279 --> 00:10:36.559
Whether mycelium building materials could
offer a solution to some of the estimated

00:10:36.559 --> 00:10:43.619
1.6 billion people worldwide who live without
adequate housing remains to be seen, but they

00:10:43.619 --> 00:10:48.829
certainly have the potential to dramatically
improve the sustainability of an industry

00:10:48.829 --> 00:10:55.439
that uses over 400 million tons of building
materials in the UK alone each year as well

00:10:55.439 --> 00:10:59.620
as creating an additional 100 million tons
of waste.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:05.009
Fungi can do all of this because their cell
walls are made of a molecule called chitin,

00:11:05.009 --> 00:11:07.360
which is bendy and tough.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:12.009
Fungi are so tough, in fact, that they help
break down rock into soil.

00:11:12.009 --> 00:11:18.410
They may also be able to help reverse the
soil damage caused by mass commercial agriculture,

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:22.730
since they're good at decomposing organic
compounds and can break down some contaminants

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:24.980
in soil, like pesticides.

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:30.990
Pollutants may also be removed from water
using mycofiltration, a process of harnessing

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:34.240
cultivated mycelium to filter water.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:40.540
If cleaning up the planet and replacing high-environmental
impact materials wasn’t enough, fungi may

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:46.559
also be playing a far greater role in preventing
climate change than was previously realised

00:11:46.559 --> 00:11:52.730
by helping trees absorb CO2 more quickly and
slowing the process of decomposition that

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:55.779
releases carbon from forest soils.

00:11:55.779 --> 00:12:01.970
These fung-tastic solutions to environmental
problems are exciting and relatively new,

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:07.069
but fungi have been powerful sources of medicine
for thousands of years, particularly in the

00:12:07.069 --> 00:12:08.069
east.

00:12:08.069 --> 00:12:13.059
There’s the Reishi mushroom, which supports
the immune system, promotes weight loss, encourages

00:12:13.059 --> 00:12:15.980
better sleep, and eases depression.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:18.799
Lion's mane mushroom is excellent for the
mind.

00:12:18.799 --> 00:12:24.079
It may even prevent Alzheimer's and multiple
sclerosis by stimulating the development of

00:12:24.079 --> 00:12:28.569
nerve growth factor, the stuff that helps
grow new neurons in the brain.

00:12:28.569 --> 00:12:33.610
Turkey tail mushroom contains a compound called
polysaccharide-K that stimulates the immune

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:34.640
system.

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:39.250
This compound is so effective in treating
cancer it's an approved prescription drug

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:40.269
in Japan.

00:12:40.269 --> 00:12:45.360
Turkey tail has been linked with resistance
to leukaemia cells, improving survival rates

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:50.399
for people with certain other cancers, and
improved immune systems for people receiving

00:12:50.399 --> 00:12:51.399
chemotherapy.

00:12:51.399 --> 00:12:56.579
There are many more examples of fungi medicine,
but Western medical science only really cottoned

00:12:56.579 --> 00:12:58.619
on to this fairly recently.

00:12:58.619 --> 00:13:04.799
In 1928, medical physician and scientist Alexander
Fleming returned from vacation to find an

00:13:04.799 --> 00:13:10.119
unusual bacteria-munching mould growing on
a petri dish in his lab.

00:13:10.119 --> 00:13:18.170
This fluffy white mass would become penicillin,
the world's first commercially-produced antibiotic.

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:23.999
Fungus is also behind the drug cyclosporine,
which helps prevent rejection of transplanted

00:13:23.999 --> 00:13:24.999
organs.

00:13:24.999 --> 00:13:32.009
An ancient and near-extinct mushroom known
as agarikon was first documented in 65 AD

00:13:32.009 --> 00:13:39.170
in Materia Medica, the first known manual
of herbal medicine, and is possibly the longest-living

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:40.779
mushroom on earth.

00:13:40.779 --> 00:13:46.119
It’s also the focus of intense medical study
for its potential in helping us fight viruses

00:13:46.119 --> 00:13:51.069
which, as you may have noticed, have been
a bit of an issue recently.

00:13:51.069 --> 00:13:56.429
Agarikon contains antiviral molecules new
to science, and initial studies show impressive

00:13:56.429 --> 00:14:01.809
activity against viruses such as pox, swine
flu, bird flu, and herpes.

00:14:01.809 --> 00:14:06.680
The fungus also has potent anti-inflammatory
and antibacterial properties.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:11.649
In ancient Greece, it was recommended in the
treatment of consumption - now known as tuberculosis

00:14:11.649 --> 00:14:17.540
- and it might prove to be a game-changer
in the fight against multidrug-resistant TB.

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:21.869
Beyond physical health, though, mushrooms
are increasingly being shown to have mental

00:14:21.869 --> 00:14:23.220
health benefits.

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:29.180
Of the tens of thousands of varieties in existence,
just a few hundred of them are known to be

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:34.779
psychoactive, which is science-speak for:
eat one and you'll trip your balls off.

00:14:34.779 --> 00:14:40.519
And most of these species have this effect
thanks to an active ingredient called psilocybin.

00:14:40.519 --> 00:14:44.920
I am, of course, referring to the fabled magic
mushroom.

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:50.290
Rock paintings in Australia suggest eating
these special fungi goes back to at least

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:56.319
10,000 BC, while more rock paintings, this
time in Spain, show prehistoric people in

00:14:56.319 --> 00:15:02.389
present-day Europe were blowing their minds
in at least 4,000 years BC.

00:15:02.389 --> 00:15:09.279
Mushroom mythology among the Mayans goes back
to 1,500 BC, and. in the 16th century, a Spanish

00:15:09.279 --> 00:15:14.850
priest called Bernardino de Sahagun wrote
about the use of magic mushrooms among the

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:16.029
Aztec people.

00:15:16.029 --> 00:15:21.480
But, when the Spanish conquered much of central
and south America, many religious rituals

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:26.249
were outlawed and magic mushrooms were forced
to go into cultural hiding.

00:15:26.249 --> 00:15:30.449
It wasn't until the mid-20th century that
they resurfaced in western countries.

00:15:30.449 --> 00:15:37.259
In 1939, two ethnobotanists named Schultes
and Reko published a paper in Harvard University

00:15:37.259 --> 00:15:43.259
Botanical Museum leaflets detailing the use
of psilocybin mushrooms by practitioners in

00:15:43.259 --> 00:15:44.259
Mexico.

00:15:44.259 --> 00:15:50.100
Just under twenty years later, two mycologists
named Wasson and Heim travelled to Mexico

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:52.290
to check the story out for themselves.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:56.879
They ate mushrooms under the guidance of a
local shaman and then wrote about the experience

00:15:56.879 --> 00:15:59.129
in Life Magazine in 1957.

00:15:59.129 --> 00:16:04.889
An editor named the piece "Seeking the Magic
Mushroom," and psychoactive mushrooms have

00:16:04.889 --> 00:16:08.670
been ‘magic’ in popular culture ever since.

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:14.170
The article was picked up by Timothy Leary,
a Harvard psychologist and big fan of hallucinogenic

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:17.459
drugs, who began to experiment with psilocybin.

00:16:17.459 --> 00:16:23.829
You might know his name from the 1968 Moody
Blues hit Legend of a Mind - apparently advocating

00:16:23.829 --> 00:16:28.779
the use of psychedelic drugs is a good way
to win brownie points with rock and roll types.

00:16:28.779 --> 00:16:33.399
Psilocybin was soon a favourite of hippies
everywhere and strongly linked with the growing

00:16:33.399 --> 00:16:36.059
counterculture of the 60s and 70s.

00:16:36.059 --> 00:16:40.089
But it wasn't all free love and psychedelic
dream catchers.

00:16:40.089 --> 00:16:44.720
Around this time, psilocybin was isolated
and synthesised by Albert Hofman, the same

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:49.290
guy who invented LSD, and used in hundreds
of clinical trials.

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:54.109
Psychologists, psychiatrists and other mental
health professionals saw promising results

00:16:54.109 --> 00:16:59.149
for patients with conditions like depression,
anxiety, alcoholism and OCD.

00:16:59.149 --> 00:17:05.510
But the 70s saw a ban placed on the use of
psilocybin for anything other than medical

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:06.510
research.

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:12.010
Studies ground to a halt, and nothing happened
for about thirty years until research started

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:13.850
up again in the 2000s.

00:17:13.850 --> 00:17:18.700
A research group at John Hopkins University
was the first to obtain regulatory approval

00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:25.530
in the US to study psilocybin, though by then
it was, in the US and many countries, a Schedule

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:31.610
1 substance, making it as illegal as heroin
or crack .But, last year, the University launched

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:37.450
the Johns Hopkins's Center for Psychedelic
and Consciousness Research dedicated to uncovering

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:41.780
the medical potential of substances like psilocybin.

00:17:41.780 --> 00:17:47.830
Using better science, more sophisticated technology
and improved methods, more and more academic

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:53.330
studies are confirming the hypothesis from
50 years ago: that the active ingredient in

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:58.360
magic mushrooms can be a powerful treatment
for multiple mental health disorders.

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:04.250
So, next time you sneer at a piece of mouldy
bread, just remember fungus could help the

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:09.770
environment, your body and your mind, and
the whole of humanity d.

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:26.820
But then throw it away anyway, because saviour
of humanity or not, mouldy bread is gross.

